-- title:
--   In dimension one every double cone is the punctured line
-- statement:
--   Work in the one-dimensional Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^1$. Let $v \in \mathbb{R}^1$ be a unit vector, $\|v\| = 1$, and let $\vartheta$ satisfy
--   $$0 < \vartheta \le \frac{\pi}{2}.$$
--   Write $V(v, \vartheta)$ for the double cone with axis $v$ and aperture $\vartheta$ — the set of vectors whose angle with the *line* spanned by $v$ is strictly less than $\vartheta$. Then
--   $$V(v, \vartheta) \;=\; \mathbb{R}^1 \setminus \{0\},$$
--   the complement of the origin.
--
--   On a line there is only one direction up to sign, so every non-zero vector is parallel to the axis and makes angle $0$ with it, which is strictly less than any positive $\vartheta$; the origin is excluded because it lies on no line through the origin in the sense the cone requires. The consequence is that in dimension one the cone structure carries no information at all: every pair of distinct points is a cone pair, which is why the one-dimensional case of the theory is degenerate and can be settled directly from the kernel bounds alone.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The unit-norm hypothesis on $v$ is what excludes the degenerate axis $v = 0$; positivity of $\vartheta$ is what makes the strict angle inequality satisfiable, and the bound $\vartheta \le \pi/2$ keeps the aperture in the admissible range. The right-hand side is written as the set-theoretic complement of the singleton $\{0\}$.
